#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a6f011-1d09-56e7-a775-9ab675b9e15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292189} {:answer/id #uuid "890b7b85-ee59-546f-a0f6-e52a7a51dab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890b7b85-ee59-546f-a0f6-e52a7a51dab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292190} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e26a32-524b-516d-b2d3-3acb9bd5f749" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c89b745-0982-5313-bdde-182c5954d58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348321" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014400/assets/e51b807bc238b140215a8a753fcfe778b14735e723dd5cb43b8aab22039a8e8e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0,$ the particle is located at the position $x=-2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=4.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(4)=-2+12=10.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52b41aa-89b5-5ed2-9c75-cbb2732e1af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52b41aa-89b5-5ed2-9c75-cbb2732e1af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313679} :answer-field/id #uuid "6440dcae-32ae-5cfc-bcf2-af7fba314d3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0fdf1b5-7a2f-532e-9de1-59360bee3559" :question/math-academy-id "q-351463" :question/problem "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={2}^{2t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=-5.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =-5+{\\int }_{1}^{3}({2}^{2t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5+{[{2}^{2t-2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-5+{2}^{2(3)-2}-{2}^{2(1)-2} \\\\  & =-5+{2}^{4}-{2}^{0} \\\\  & =-5+16-1 \\\\  & =-5+15 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0270f788-1cdb-528c-85e3-c5e8dda56681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292216} {:answer/id #uuid "53db8982-8e73-5277-90aa-165c3b52a5d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292217} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ed76bd-8402-55cd-8079-0e89ab2fe6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292218} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f2dd82-6236-5f56-9bce-6792b0bf2373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292219} {:answer/id #uuid "237c114d-eacb-5eb4-8963-fe8655d0f624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0270f788-1cdb-528c-85e3-c5e8dda56681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292216} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c76b68b-b8cf-58ee-b229-494f9f0c8abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1394edaf-31dd-59b4-a755-2b8323ac548f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81506" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3+([\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]) \\\\  & =3+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =3+(0-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd2c0fd5-d417-5242-890a-9ba274bf8aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292223} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ad5343-d3a0-5ef8-8e06-0b5a445ca0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292224} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a015ad-6d6f-54f6-a0fa-4049989b6947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292225} {:answer/id #uuid "e25f6d64-c605-522c-86f4-c3f86038592a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292226} {:answer/id #uuid "743493e3-075b-53e2-af57-e10c534f77b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd2c0fd5-d417-5242-890a-9ba274bf8aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292223} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc57005-e790-5437-a053-97dc77545aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5341cef1-205c-5e6a-a5f6-442c5b5b09a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81509" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014400/assets/b8e6f6b7ab2081595723002737bcb73bbced52bfea0f8cfeb91b1201adab0160.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=-2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=-2+9=7\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=7.$"}]}
